Marvel Comics hasn't always been as big  as it is today and not all of the  company's history is as heroic as The  Adventures of spider-man or Captain  America since starting out as timely  comics in 1939 they've created countless  classics but they've also done a few  things they probably wish we'd forget  here are a few of Marvel's biggest  misstep s-- atlas distribution disaster  back in the 1950s when Marvel was still  called Atlas comics publisher Martin  Goodman decided to outsource  distribution one problem the  distribution company almost immediately  went bankrupt leaving Goodman no option  but to ask archrival DC to distribute  his books DC put the screws to Atlas  limiting them to only eight comics a  month a huge drop from the 60 or so  titles Atlas had previously published  not only did profits plunge but Atlas  had to fire almost all of their artists  and writers there was simply nothing for  them to do ironically the company  survived because of an editorial  oversight by Stan Lee for years he had  been buying too many stories from his  creators ending up with a closet full of  unpublished comics when Atlas imploded  they were able to stay afloat by finally  publishing the issues Stan had  accidently stockpiled tossing pages  before comics were seen as collectibles  original comic art was a means to an end  it was photographed colored printed and  then the originals were discarded while  some original pages have been preserved  by collectors Marvel spent decades  simply chucking countless others not  unlike the way the BBC tossed dozens of  early Doctor Who episodes today finding  a missing comic page is a victory for  both collectors and comics historians  creator mistreatment it's a sad fact  that while Marvel rakes in billions of  dollars worldwide with their films the  creators often see little or no money  from it thanks to work for hire  contracts that sign all the rights away  one of the worst examples of Marvel's  questionable treatment of their creators  is the case of Gary Friedrich the  co-creator of Ghost Rider after the  Ghost Rider movies came out Friedrich  took Marvel to court for compensation  related to merchandising not only did he  lose the lawsuit Marvel counter sued for  $17,000 and forced him to stop selling  anything ghostwriter related at  conventions a judge later overturned the  decision and both parties settled out of  court  Royale Roy  for a period Marvel Comics also  published star comics label dedicated to  animated tie-ins like Thundercats and  Fraggle Rock and other kid-friendly fare  part of stars approach was to copy the  success of Harvey comics they did a  little too well though royal Roy a young  millionaire was basically an exact copy  of Harvey's Richie Rich and was actually  created by an ex Harvey employee Harvey  sued Marvel for infringing on their  property and royal Roy was cancelled  after a handful of issues Ron Pearlman  in January of 1989 Ron Perelman bought  Marvel for just over 82 million dollars  he then went full-on wolf of Wall Street  using Marvel as collateral to leverage  the purchase of other trading card comic  book and toy companies sending the  company 700 million dollars into death  by the time pro man's reign ended the  company had declared bankruptcy over a  third of its employees had been laid off  and the entire comic book industry had  collapsed and Pearlman while he got rich  off of it all with an estimated net  worth of 14 billion dollars sometimes  the super villains win film rights in  the early 90s cash-strapped Marvel  raised capital by signing away the film  rights to pretty much all their  characters for pennies on the dollar  it seems short-sighted but it almost  makes sense given that every Marvel film  prior to 1998 blade was a failure don't  remember those films there's a good  reason for that first there was a 1990  direct-to-video version of Captain  America which reported profits of just  over 10 thousand dollars on a 10 million  dollar budget  then four years later the first  Fantastic Four was produced on a  shoestring just so a small movie studio  could legally retain the rights to the  characters it was so bad Marvel ended up  spending millions of dollars to keep the  film from being distributed in order to  save the brand from being cheapened by a  low-budget flop Marvel learned their  lesson though and in 2004 they formed  their own studio and began buying back  the rights to their characters so they  can make the movies themselves seems  like that move worked out pretty well  Marvel's image in 1992 several of  Marvel's top creators went to their  bosses and asked for a better deal more  money per page more royalties for  characters they created and a more fair  portion of what Marvel was earning off  of their work Marvel told them to pounce  and in response the artists quit Marvel  and form their own company Image Comics  they became super stars an image went on  to revolutionize the industry while  Marvel stock dropped so sharply it was  featured on an episode of money line  Marvel survived but they could have  stayed ahead of the game and saved  themselves a lot of trouble just by  playing fair guiding light in 2006  Marvel's editor-in-chief Joe Quesada  thought it would be a great idea to  partner with of all companies Procter &amp;  Gamble mostly known for selling Pringles  creating the bowel loosening food  additive olestra and producing the soap  opera guiding light to that end guiding  light aired an episode in which a  longtime character was shocked by some  Halloween decorations and briefly gained  superpowers Marvel wrote their own  version of the story as a six-page mini  crossover the hope was that the  cross-promotion would encourage soap  viewers and comics fans to walk on the  other side but not many did and guiding  light only lasted a few more years  before it was canceled  dang thanks for watching subscribe to  our YouTube channel to watch more videos  like the one you just saw and leave us a  comment to let us know which Marvel  mistake you think was the worst
 

